@Petrol

Here is some more etymological trivia for you: the Polish word for shop, sklep, comes from sklepienie, [vaulted] ceiling. Historically most people shopped at stalls (stragany, apparently derived from the German Schrage). The first shops usually opened in the cellars and ground floors of buildings, and these spaces were often vaulted (sklepione). The generic term for a vaulted room (sklep) in time came to mean any permanent vending establishment.

Also, the old Polish word for a store, skład, literally means a store - "składać" translates to "to store".

